  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 441 AM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nDry today into this evening with high pressure at the surface and \nupper level ridging remain in place. Expect partly to mostly sunny \nskies and highs around 80 degrees. Patchy fog is possible this \nmorning, with a few locations showing less than 1 mile visibility. \nDon't expect any widespread dense fog, and suspect it's fairly \nshallow. If lower visibilities are reported persistently at a \ngreater number of stations, may issue an SPS to raise awareness for \nthose on the morning commute. \n\nA trough moves through later tonight into Saturday as the upper \nlevel ridge meanders to the NE CONUS. Warm air/moisture advection \nkicks in overnight on the LLJ, bringing additional moisture to the \narea, especially further east. We could see showers with the warm \nfront tonight into early Sat, and then again during the afternoon \nhours as a cold front (associated with a low pressure system moving \ninto Hudson Bay) moves into our western CWA. The front then crosses \ninto the overnight hours, settling just south and east of our CWA by \nmorning. Suspect the best chances will be along/south of US 24, \nespecially east of I 69. For now have 20-40 percent chances in this \narea, with a few thunderstorms possible Sat afternoon. Kept Sat PM \ndry for now, but if front moves through slower we could see pops \nshift further north and west and extend into Sat evening. Highs \nSaturday will be in the low-mid 80s (a few upper 80s west of IN-15). \nSevere weather is not expected at this time. \n\nAnother shortwave could move through somewhat zonal/slightly \ntroughed flow on Sunday, perhaps lighting up convection along the \nfrontal zone to the south/east of our CWA. For now have our area \ndry, but if the front is slower to exit we could see chances for \nshowers/isolated storms return for areas along/south of US 24. \nOtherwise, on Sunday we'll have breezy northerly winds (especially \nnear Lake MI), so expect breaking waves of 2-3 feet and potentially \nstrong currents along SE Lake MI beaches. Accordingly, have a \nmoderate swim risk forecasted for both La Porte Co, IN/Berrien Co, \nMI. \n\nMonday into early Tuesday afternoon should be mostly dry as an upper \nlevel ridge/surface high pressure build in over the Great Lakes. \nHighs will be in the mid-upper 70s, low-mid 80s. The best chances \nfor any precipitation will be later Tue afternoon into Thursday (30-\n65 percent). An upper level trough (potentially a closed low) will \ndig into the western edge of the exiting ridge during this period, \nwith a surface low drifting from the northern plains Monday \nafternoon into northern Ontario/Hudson Bay by Tuesday afternoon. The \ntrailing cold front will attempt to push through sometime Tuesday \nafternoon/evening and then stalls somewhere over our CWA-becoming a \nfocus for more persistent activity through Thursday. The models \ndisagree on the strength/exact position of the upper level ridge and \nwhether or not the upper low remains and deepens (resulting in a \nstronger surface low) or if it will weaken and fill, resulting in a \nweaker surface system. It's possible we stay dry until late Tuesday \nif the weaker low materializes and/or the ridge is stronger than \nforecast. We could see chances as early as Tuesday morning/early \nafternoon if the stronger upper low/surface system develops and/or \nthe ridge is weaker than forecast and moves further SE. For now, \nhave 30-50 percent chances Tue, and 40-60 percent chances Tue night \ninto Thursday as the cold front moves in and stalls over our\narea. Thunderstorms are possible Monday night then again Tuesday\nafternoon into Thursday, especially during the afternoon hours.\nIf the front makes it in, we'll see gusty southwest winds shift\nnorth through the afternoon/evening, with waves potentially up \nto 4 feet on Lake MI beaches in La Porte Co, IN/Berrien Co, MI. \nKept a moderate swim risk for now given lower confidence (2-3 ft\nwaves), but definitely worth keeping an eye on for a potential \nbeach hazards statement/high swim risk. \n\nHighs Tue will be in the mid-upper 80s (especially if drier model \nsolutions are correct), and then remain in the upper 70s, low-mid \n80s for Wed-Thu. Friday will be coolest, with highs in the 70s.",
